    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the number of ways to arrange 3 math books and 5 English books such that all the math books are together and all the English books are together, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Treat the math books as a single unit and the English books as a single unit:**\n   Since the math books must stay together and the English books must stay together, we can consider each group as a single \"super book.\" This means we have 2 \"super books\" to arrange on the shelf.\n   \n   The number of ways to arrange 2 \"super books\" is given by the factorial of 2:\n   \\[\n   2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n   \\]\n   So, there are 2 ways to arrange the 2 \"super books\" on the shelf.\n\n2. **Arrange the books within each \"super book\":**\n   - The 3 math books can be arranged among themselves in \\(3!\\) (3 factorial) ways.\n   \\[\n   3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n   - The 5 English books can be arranged among themselves in \\(5!\\) (5 factorial) ways.\n   \\[\n   5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n   \\]\n\n3. **Combine the arrangements:**\n   The total number of ways to arrange the books is the product of the number of ways to arrange the 2 \"super books\" and the number of ways to arrange the books within each \"super book\":\n   \\[\n   2! \\times 3! \\times 5! = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120 = 1440\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf such that all the math books are together and all the English books are together is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
