    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to consider the constraints given: all the math books must stay together and all the English books must also stay together. This means we can treat the math books as a single unit and the English books as another single unit. So, we are essentially arranging two units (the group of math books and the group of English books) on the shelf.\n\n1. **Treat the groups as single units:**\n   - There are 2 units: one unit containing the 3 math books and the other unit containing the 5 English books.\n   - We need to determine the number of ways to arrange these 2 units.\n   - The number of ways to arrange 2 units is given by the factorial of 2, which is \\(2!\\).\n\n2. **Calculate the number of arrangements:**\n   \\[\n   2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Arrange the books within each unit:**\n   - The 3 math books can be arranged amongst themselves in \\(3!\\) ways.\n   - The 5 English books can be arranged amongst themselves in \\(5!\\) ways.\n\n4. **Calculate the arrangements within each unit:**\n   \\[\n   3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n   \\]\n\n5. **Combine the arrangements:**\n   - The total number of ways to arrange the units and the books within each unit is the product of the number of ways to arrange the units and the number of ways to arrange the books within each unit.\n   \\[\n   2! \\times 3! \\times 5! = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120\n   \\]\n\n6. **Perform the multiplication:**\n   \\[\n   2 \\times 6 = 12\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   12 \\times 120 = 1440\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books on the shelf, with all math books together and all English books together, is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
